Subject: workflow using nimbleModel(calculate = T/F) and model$calculate()
Message-ID: <oLoqQ6qMHLM.8zUEUd4hBAAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/nimble-users/c/oLoqQ6qMHLM/m/8zUEUd4hBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Following initial conversations and guidance from folks here, I have developed a Nimble workflow as follows:

1. Write model
2. Use `nimbleModel()` to define and build the model
3. Run `model$calculate()` to check that I get a number for the log-likelihood and thus the model is fully initialized, specified, and is set to start in a defined portion of parameter space
4. Initiate sampling

During step 2, I am noticing "Running calculate on model" in the R console, which made me wonder if step 3 is duplicating what is already done in step 2. I then searched here and found this post <https://groups.google.com/g/nimble-users/c/Wl98yAfZzfQ/m/7nZYLJO_AAAJ>, where Perry advises the questioner to set calculate = FALSE when running nimbleModel(). I have two questions:

1. Is it possible to get the calculated overall deviance from the model object produced by `nimbleModel()` without having to run Step 3?
2. Alternatively, should I always be setting `nimbleModel(calculate = FALSE)` to avoid wasting time on what I will be doing in step 3?
3. Moreover, in line 40 of my function that parallelizes Nimble here <https://github.com/Bird-Conservancy-of-the-Rockies/FunctionsBCR/blob/main/R/RunNimbleParallel.r>, should I be setting calculate = FALSE to avoid wasting time when compiling the model, or is this in fact a needed step when setting up the model prior to compiling samplers?

