Hi All, just following up on this thread for the sake of anyone in the future who might be interested in the same question. In the end the code above basically worked, except that we got rid of the 'discrete = TRUE' option. Discrete distributions apparently assume integer values, whereas I was looking for output values from 0 to 1 (albeit binned in my custom distribution probability table, hence the initial use of the discrete option).

Another issue that came up where I was converting a proportion value (e.g., 0.29) to a row number by multiplying by 100. Many are probably already familiar with this, but it was new to me. Because not all fractions can be stored exactly in R, the following line of code returns the value '28' instead of the desired '29'. This is because the value of 29/100*100 is actually slightly less than 29, and when indexing 28.9999 returns the 28th (rather than 29th) element.

>(1:30)[29/100*100]
28

So rather than the old
prob <- probabilityArray[(round(x,2)*100)+1,i,j,k]

I now use:
prob <- probabilityArray[round(x*100)+1,i,j,k]

So, the moral of the story for me was to always round indexing values if they've been derived from non-integer values anywhere along the way! Probably old hat to many.

Finally, in defining the custom distributions, rather than use my empirically derived raw probabilities for values in each 0.01-width bin, I used a smoothing function to make them less erratic. As an example, here are my raw (dots) and smoothed (line) probabilities for a given instance:

Thanks again Daniel!

-Ryan

